course: everyday
lesson: 480
title: The friend in the game you never met, and the fake friend
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Connor has played every night for months with «xXShadowWolfXx», who is funny, gives tips and gifted him a skin (an outfit for your character). Then: «what's ur home address, bro, wanna send you a proper present». Connor tells his dad. His dad blocks and reports the account and turns on the privacy settings. Connor is gutted to lose his gaming mate, and a bit scared. ShadowWolf could have been anyone. A gift followed by a question about where you live is a warning sign.
explain: The biggest red flag is a secret from your parents. Mia's Roblox friend «Bella_10» says she's ten and lives in Florida, then asks to video chat: «just us, don't tell ur mom, she'd be weird about it». Sam's online chess friend says, «Our chats are special. Your parents wouldn't get it.» A girl Mia met in a game says, «Run away and come live with me, I'll send a car.» Each time the child told a grown-up, the account was reported, and the police took the worst one seriously and thanked Mia. Safe people never ask you to keep secrets from your parents, or to meet up or get in a car.
explain: People online can pretend to be anyone. «Dev_11» says he's eleven, but his voice is deep, and one day his camera flicks on by mistake: a grown man. Jake leaves and tells his dad. A «free Robux» guy promises Kieran 10,000 Robux (game money) for a photo «to prove you're real». Kieran tells his mum, and the police officer says he did exactly the right thing. Chloe gets a request from «ur cousin Niamh's mate»; she texts Niamh: «Who? Never heard of her.» Block and report.
explain: Some online friends are real. Lily's mum checks that «sketchy_scot» is a real girl in Inverness, and the girls swap drawings by post. «It's pure dreich here,» the letters say, and Lily learns dreich is Scottish for grey and drizzly. Maddie in Perth plays Minecraft with her cousin Aoife in Galway: Aoife says «the craic was mighty» (Irish for great fun) and Maddie says «sweet as» (Aussie for great). And when a stranger calls Connor «noob» and «trash» in voice chat, he mutes him and reports him after the match.
fact: Someone you only know online might not be who they say they are; people can lie about their name, age and where they live.
fact: Never give your address, school, phone number or photos to someone you only know online.
fact: A gift in a game followed by a request for personal details is a warning sign.
fact: Anyone online who asks you to keep secrets from your parents is not safe; tell a grown-up straight away.
fact: Anyone online who asks you to meet up, run away or get in a car is dangerous; tell a grown-up at once.
fact: Anyone online offering you things in exchange for a photo is not a friend; stop, don't reply, and tell an adult.
fact: Telling a grown-up is right even if the online person has been kind for a long time.
fact: Blocking stops someone contacting you; reporting tells the game or app so they can act.
fact: «Friend of a friend» requests are easy to fake, so check with the real friend first.
fact: Playing online with people you already know in real life is the safest kind.
fact: Some online friends are real, but a parent should check before anything moves outside the game.
fact: You don't have to listen to strangers being rude in voice chat: mute and report.
fact: «Dreich» is Scottish for grey and drizzly; «craic» is Irish for fun; «sweet as» is Australian and New Zealand for great.
exercise: What did ShadowWolf ask Connor for after gifting him a skin? | His home address | His favourite colour | His high score | His game tips
exercise: What did Connor's dad do? | Blocked and reported the account and set privacy settings | Sent ShadowWolf a message telling him to come round and talk | Bought Connor a new game | Told Connor to keep playing but never use the voice chat again
exercise: Bella_10 asked to video chat. What part was the biggest red flag? | «Don't tell ur mom» | That she lives in Florida | That she plays Roblox | That she says she's only ten
exercise: The «free Robux» guy wanted what? | A photo of Kieran | Kieran's password only | A tip for the next game level | Kieran's favourite song
exercise: What did the police tell Kieran? | He did exactly the right thing | He was in big trouble for telling | He should have sent the photo first | Robux are free anyway, so no harm was done
exercise: How did Lily's mum make the drawing friendship safe? | She checked the girl was real and the mums agreed | She gave the girl their home address for letters | She banned all drawing | She pretended to be Lily in every letter she sent back
exercise: What did Jake see when Dev_11's camera flicked on? | A grown man | A boy of eleven | A dog on the sofa | Nothing, it was dark
exercise: How did Chloe check the «friend of Niamh» request? | She texted her real cousin Niamh | She accepted and asked questions | She sent a photo | She asked the stranger to prove it
exercise: What did Sam's chess friend say that was a warning sign? | Keep our chats secret from your parents | Let's play another game | You're really good at chess for your age | Good game, well played, see you tomorrow
exercise: Who does Maddie play Minecraft with? | Her cousin Aoife, who she knows in real life | A stranger called ShadowWolf from the forum | A man pretending to be ten | Nobody, she always plays all on her own
exercise: A stranger in voice chat kept calling Connor «noob». What did he do? | Muted him and reported him after the match | Shouted back worse names | Gave up gaming forever | Sent the stranger his address
exercise: A girl from a game said «run away, I'll send a car». What did Mia do? | Told her mum and teacher, who called the police | Packed a bag and waited by the front gate | Kept it secret | Asked what colour the car would be and when it was coming
exercise: Why did Connor feel gutted after his dad blocked ShadowWolf? | He'd lost someone he thought was a mate | He'd lost all his games | He was banned from the internet | His dad was cross with him
exercise: What does «dreich» mean in Scotland? | Grey and drizzly | Very hot and sunny | Delicious | Far, far away
exercise: What is the difference between blocking and reporting? | Blocking stops contact, reporting tells the game | They are exactly the same thing with two names | Reporting deletes your own account | Blocking calls the police and reporting tells your mum
exercise: What does «sweet as» mean in Australian slang? | Great | Very sugary | Too sad | Very slowly
test: «he's been my gaming bro for months, he's legit, I can give him my address». Safe? | No, never give your address to an online-only friend | Yes, months of gaming together proves he's real and safe | Yes, if he gave you a skin | Yes, if he says he's ten and goes to a school near you
test: «don't tell ur mom lol, she wouldn't get it». What does this message tell you? | This person is not safe, tell a grown-up | They're a good friend who keeps your secrets safe | Your mum is too old for games | It's a normal thing that friends say all the time
test: «10k Robux for one pic, no cap, easy money». What should Kieran do? | Stop, don't reply, and tell an adult | Send one quick photo and then block him | Ask for 20k first | Send a photo of his dog to trick him
test: Jake: «his voice is mad deep but he says he's 11, maybe he's got a cold». What's wiser? | Trust the funny feeling and tell a grown-up | Believe whatever he says | Ask him to turn his camera on to prove it | Give him your phone number so you can check his voice
test: «hiya I'm ur cousin's mate add me x». What's the smart first step? | Check with the real cousin | Accept, cousins' mates are fine | Send her your school name | Ask her to send a photo
test: What would probably happen if Mia had kept the «run away, I'll send a car» chat secret? | She could be in real danger | The girl would leave her alone | Nothing at all | She'd get a new best friend
test: «all online friends are fake, so I'll never play online again». Is that right? | No, some are real, but let a parent check | Yes, every single online person is fake | Yes, games are banned for kids | No, so you can trust everyone you meet online
test: «u got merked, noob, go cry to ur mommy». (merked means beaten badly.) What's best? | Mute him, report him, keep playing | Cry and quit the game for good | Tell him your real name | Insult him back for an hour until he leaves
test: In a cartoon, a kid sneaks out to meet an internet friend and it's an adventure. What's true in real life? | Meeting an online stranger can be very dangerous | It's always a fun adventure, just like on TV | Online friends are always kids | Parents always come along secretly to keep watch
test: ShadowWolf was nice for months. Does that mean it was safe to tell Connor's dad? | Yes, telling is right even if they've been nice | No, nice people should never be reported | No, Connor should have asked ShadowWolf first | Yes, but only if ShadowWolf was rude
test: Maddie plays Minecraft with her cousin in Ireland every week. Why is that safer than with ShadowWolf? | She knows her cousin in real life | Ireland has much better internet | Minecraft has no strangers in it at all | Cousins never lie about anything
test: «why's my mum reading the letters from the Scottish girl, so cringe». Why does she check? | To make sure the friend is real and safe | Because she's nosy and bored | To keep the best drawings for herself | Because Scotland is a dangerous place to write to
exam: Which of the following requests from a person known solely through an online game constitutes the gravest indication of danger? | A request that the child conceal the communication from her parents | A request to play a further game | An offer of advice concerning game strategy and improved technique | A compliment upon the child's performance in the preceding match
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate conduct of a child invited by an online acquaintance to leave home and enter a vehicle sent for her. | To inform a parent or teacher without delay | To comply with the invitation discreetly | To request further particulars of the vehicle | To keep the invitation confidential
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the representations of age made by persons online. | Such persons may misrepresent their age and identity | Such representations are invariably accurate | Persons online are prevented from lying by law | Representations of age are verified by every game before play begins
exam: Identify the principal distinction between blocking and reporting an online account. | Blocking prevents contact, whereas reporting informs the platform | Blocking informs the police, whereas reporting deletes one's own account | There exists no distinction between the two | Reporting prevents contact, whereas blocking has no effect whatsoever
